From fab6faf8e093ee59d2ff7f133fae11c62c58565a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: n0madic <800462+n0madic@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 23 Oct 2026 11:55:43 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] Let a polling guest see every timer count

Soulcalibur III calibrates its frame period at start-up: it counts
Timer 0 (BUSCLK/256) over 256 changes of Timer 3 (scan lines), reading
Timer 3 once and then until it changes. A timer read let the EE clock
jump to host time, so with the host behind, Timer 3 skipped lines between
two reads and the measured Timer 0 rate came out 1.6-2x too high. The
frame period grew to ~1.5-2 fields, so the game took two-field frames
for on time and never used its frame skip: the battle ran at half speed.

- A timer read lets the clock catch up by at most 128 EE cycles, about
  what one poll iteration costs, so a poll sees every count.
- Code that runs long without reading a timer must not hold the VBlank
  back, so a time slice ends with the clock caught up with host time,
  unless the guest read a timer during the slice.
